---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "../lib/url";
import meet from "../../config/meet.json";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Cost, Travel & FAQ"
  description="Honest, practical answers about what Western Zones is, what the week looks like, and what it costs."
>
  <h1 class="page-title">Cost, Travel &amp; FAQ</h1>
  <p class="page-subtitle">
    Straight answers, including the ones we don't have yet. If something here is wrong or out of
    date, tell us.
  </p>

  <div class="card">
    <h2 class="card-heading">What is Zones, really?</h2>
    <p>
      The <strong>Western Zone Age Group Championships</strong> is the biggest meet most 14&amp;U
      swimmers in the West can reach. Every LSC in the Western Zone — Arizona among them — sends a
      team, and swimmers compete for their <strong>LSC</strong>, not their club.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      For a lot of kids it's the first time they wear "Arizona" on a cap instead of their club name,
      the first time they travel with a team, and the first time they see what a fast heat really
      looks like. Ask anyone who's been.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2 class="card-heading">Who can go?</h2>
    <p>A swimmer needs all three:</p>
    <ol class="tight-list">
      <li>Registered with <strong>Arizona Swimming</strong>.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>14 or younger on the first day of competition</strong> — not today, and not on their
        club's roster. Age on day one of the meet is what counts, and it's what puts a swimmer in
        10&amp;U, 11-12, or 13-14.
      </li>
      <li>At least one <strong>AAA time</strong> in any course during the qualifying window.</li>
    </ol>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      Not sure? The <a href={withBase("/calculator")}>calculator</a> will tell you in about ten
      seconds.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card callout callout-warn">
    <h2 class="card-heading">Does a AAA time mean my swimmer is going?</h2>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      <strong>No.</strong> This is the single most important thing on this page. Being eligible means
      a swimmer <em>could</em> be selected. Arizona Swimming selects the team, by its own process, and
      the Zone caps the roster at {meet.rosterCap} athletes per LSC. Eligibility is the door being
      open — walking through it is up to the LSC and your coach.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2 class="card-heading">What does it cost?</h2>
    <p class="note">
      We don't know, and we'd rather say so than guess. Arizona Swimming does not currently publish a
      team fee for Zones. Once it does, it will go here.
    </p>
    <p>Realistically, families should expect some mix of:</p>
    <ul class="tight-list">
      <li>A team fee set by the LSC (covers coaching, team gear, and staff costs)</li>
      <li>Travel to the host city — which for {meet.targetYear} is <strong>{meet.location}</strong></li>
      <li>Lodging for roughly a week, often at a designated team hotel</li>
      <li>Meals, and a parent's own travel if they're going</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      <strong>If cost is the thing standing in the way, ask.</strong> Clubs and LSCs frequently have
      travel assistance, and it is very often underused simply because families don't know to ask for
      it. Talk to your coach early — well before the entry deadline, not after.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2 class="card-heading">Do parents travel with the team?</h2>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      Usually the team travels together with LSC-appointed coaches and chaperones, and families
      travel separately if they want to watch. Chaperone ratios and Safe Sport requirements are set
      by the LSC. Your coach can tell you how Arizona has handled it in past years.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2 class="card-heading">What does the week actually look like?</h2>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      Four days of competition, prelims in the morning and finals at night, with the team together
      between sessions. Swimmers can enter up to <strong>6 individual events</strong>, maximum
      <strong>3 per day</strong>, plus relays. It's a long week and a fast one.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2 class="card-heading">I think my swimmer qualifies. What now?</h2>
    <ol class="tight-list">
      <li>Check the time on the <a href={withBase("/calculator")}>calculator</a>.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Talk to your club coach.</strong> They know the LSC process, the deadlines, and
        whether the time was swum in an approved meet.
      </li>
      <li>Watch for Arizona Swimming's announcement and the entry deadline — <strong>{meet.entryDeadline}</strong> for {meet.targetYear}.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note" style="margin-bottom:0">
      Don't wait for someone to contact you. Plenty of eligible swimmers never go simply because
      nobody realized they were eligible in time.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
